{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they said to Moses and to Elazar the priest and to the princes of the congregation.</b> It is necessary to know why it was not sufficient for them to speak to Moses alone, and if there is no need for the matter, why did the verse need to mention them? Perhaps the intention is to say that the matter was done in the manner that HaShem commanded regarding the inheritance of the land, as it is written in parashat Eleh Masei (Numbers 34:17): ״אֵלֶּה אֲשֶׁר יִנְחֲלוּ לָכֶם אֶת הָאָרֶץ אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן נוּן וְנָשִׂיא אֶחָד נָשִׂיא אֶחָד״, etc. — that a king and a High Priest and the princes are required, and these are the ones mentioned here. And the reason that HaShem required all three is that each one has a different authority. Regarding the king, you will find that the Rambam wrote in chapter 4 of Hilkhot Melakhim, and this is his language: ״All the land that he conquers belongs to him, and he gives it to his servants and to the men of war as he sees fit,״ end quote. You see that the king has everything in his hand. Elazar and the princes — when the king does not wish to give except to each one what is fitting for him according to the division, all the recipients of the portions need to be present for the division. And the priest, to know with which place El Elyon will agree that so-and-so should take his portion. You have thus learned that all three are required to be in agreement for the giving of a portion in the land; each one has a different authority. And moreover, there is reason to suggest that the law of a king who gives to whomever he wishes applies only after the division of the land and the settlement of Israel in their cities, but not at the beginning of the inheritance, when the king does not have this prerogative; therefore the children of Gad and the children of Reuben needed to stand also before Elazar and the princes. And its saying <b>saying</b> — perhaps it intends that they say to all Israel a matter that they would agree upon. Or it may intend a soft saying, the language of appeasement."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:31.522255744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}